Hi [~accountid:712020:13033e65-435c-4794-a380-e007eba7615a] - thank you; I am generally aligned with the ideas below, however, I have the following comments:

{quote}_As above, there will always be a risk that these comms are interpreted as marketing that requires driver consent - it is unlikely they would be classified as transactional (regardless of the phrasing). However, the risk of drivers actually complaining about these comms is lower, given that they are essentially telling drivers that they have saved money (which should be received positively)._{quote}

{color:#bf2600}Let’s make it clear that even with the risk-reducing measures the comms would still have to be categorised as marketing (i.e. {color}{color:#bf2600}_Bolt_{color}{color:#bf2600} products and services), but not third party marketing.{color}

{quote}* _IMO, taking the Bolt Delivery approach (i.e., promoting exclusive offers of merchants/products that are being sold in Bolt app) is too risky here. The average complainant would interpret this as marketing for which consent is required, and so I think this could create a few headaches for us in the long term if we too readily allow it here._{quote}

{color:#bf2600}I believe the likelihood of complaints regarding these communications is similar to any other marketing. The main risk arises from the impact of complainants escalating to regulators.{color}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
{color:#bf2600}I suggest adding a general point that our proposed strategy allows us to promote “Partner Offers” with generally low risk. Higher-risk strategies, which involve promoting specific third-party bundle deals, carry greater risk but remain an option for the business if we can accept the potential for regulatory escalations.{color}
{panel}

